d " style="transform:rotate(0.10000000000000009deg)">Flagged</span></div><p class="mt-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted [overflow-wrap:anywhere]">This server claims to validate, transform, and normalize structured data for AI agents, which is a common and legitimate utility category. It would be relevant to agent developers who need schema cleanup and data-shaping in tool pipelines. The repository namespace strongly resembles a misspelled version of a well-known firm name, which is a clear namespace-squat signal and outweighs the otherwise plausible description.</p></article><article class="min-w-0 rounded-card border border-border bg-paper-raised/40 px-5 py-4"><div class="flex min-w-0 flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-2"><h3 class="min-w-0 font-display text-base font-semibold leading-snug"><a href="/servers/io-github-kyletaggart-lsp" class="break-words text-ink no-underline hover:text-dead">lsp</a> <span class="meta-mono ml-1 break-all !normal-case">io.github.KyleTaggart/lsp</span></h3><span class="stamp stamp-dead " style="transform:rotate(0deg)">Flagged</span></div><p class="mt-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted [overflow-wrap:anywhere]">This appears to be an asset-sync server for moving creative files from tools like Lightroom, Figma, Dropbox, and Canva into WordPress and Shopify. That could fit agencies or ecommerce content teams, but the package name &#x27;lsp&#x27; is so generic and mismatched to the described function that it raises a namespace-squat concern. With no other maturity signal present, this entry warrants extra scrutiny.</p></article><article class="min-w-0 rounded-card border border-border bg-paper-raised/40 px-5 py-4"><div class="flex min-w-0 flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-2"><h3 class="min-w-0 font-display text-base font-semibold leading-snug"><a href="/servers/com-codeniverse-mcp" class="break-words text-ink no-underline hover:text-dead">mcp</a> <span class="meta-mono ml-1 break-all !norma